<!-- =====================================================
     PHASE 12 BANNER
     ===================================================== -->
<div class="p12-banner">
    <div class="p12-phase-pill">Phase 12 · Medium Idle Engine + CVT Bridge + Full Thermal Recovery Stack</div>
    <h1>The Cold-Valley Fix — <em>Medium Idle + Transmission Compensation + Exhaust Heat Harvest</em></h1>
    <p>
        Phase 12 addresses the fundamental flaw identified across Phases 10–11: the generator cannot sustain
        the boiler at low idle RPM. The solution is architectural — <strong>stop designing for low idle</strong>.
        The engine now idles at a medium-high RPM (~2,000 generator-equivalent), and a
        <strong>Static CVT Transmission</strong> sits between the engine output and the drivetrain,
        absorbing and compensating the difference so the mechanical output to wheels/rails remains smooth
        regardless of what the engine is doing internally. Simultaneously, eight thermal recovery mechanisms
        are stacked to harvest every watt of waste energy the system produces — with the
        <strong>Eddy Current Exhaust Pipe Recovery</strong> as the headline new addition.
    </p>
</div>

<!-- =====================================================
     SECTION 1: THE NEW IDLE ARCHITECTURE
     ===================================================== -->
<div class="p12-section">

    <div class="p12-section-label">Phase 12 · Core Architectural Shift</div>
    <h2 class="p12-section-title">The Medium Idle Solution — Don't fight the cold valley, raise the floor above it</h2>

    <p class="p12-section-lead">
        In Phase 11, the engine was designed to idle at ~20 PSI / ~120 RPM — too slow for the generator to
        self-sustain. Phase 12 reframes the entire problem: the boiler minimum operating pressure is raised to
        ~90 PSI (medium idle), which keeps the static engine's internal rotors at ~500 RPM equivalent.
        At that RPM, the generator in low gear already exceeds the 4,200W boiler demand.
        The CVT transmission ensures that this medium idle does NOT translate to high output speed
        at the drivetrain — it absorbs the difference, outputting whatever torque/speed the operator commands.
    </p>

    <div class="p12-idle-concept-grid">
        <div class="p12-concept-card p12-card-problem">
            <div class="p12-concept-badge p12-badge-red">Phase 10–11 Problem</div>
            <h3>Low Idle → Generator Deficit</h3>
            <p>Engine designed to idle at 20 PSI / ~120 RPM. Generator at that speed produces only ~640W. Boiler needs 4,200W. Deficit of −3,560W drains battery. No thermal recovery closes this gap alone.</p>
            <div class="p12-concept-stat"><span>Idle RPM (engine equiv.)</span><span class="p12-stat-red">~120 RPM</span></div>
            <div class="p12-concept-stat"><span>Generator at idle</span><span class="p12-stat-red">~640W</span></div>
            <div class="p12-concept-stat"><span>Boiler demand</span><span class="p12-stat-red">−4,200W</span></div>
            <div class="p12-concept-stat"><span>Net deficit</span><span class="p12-stat-red">−3,560W</span></div>
        </div>

        <div class="p12-concept-card p12-card-arrow">
            <div class="p12-arrow-symbol">→</div>
            <div class="p12-arrow-label">Phase 12 Shift</div>
        </div>

        <div class="p12-concept-card p12-card-solution">
            <div class="p12-concept-badge p12-badge-green">Phase 12 Solution</div>
            <h3>Medium Idle → Generator Surplus</h3>
            <p>Engine minimum idle raised to 90 PSI / ~500 RPM equiv. Generator in low gear now produces ~4,800W. CVT absorbs excess mechanical energy and smooths drivetrain output. Boiler self-sustains from cold start.</p>
            <div class="p12-concept-stat"><span>Idle RPM (engine equiv.)</span><span class="p12-stat-green">~500 RPM</span></div>
            <div class="p12-concept-stat"><span>Generator at idle</span><span class="p12-stat-green">~4,800W</span></div>
            <div class="p12-concept-stat"><span>Boiler demand</span><span class="p12-stat-gold">−2,800W (vacuum jacket)</span></div>
            <div class="p12-concept-stat"><span>Net surplus</span><span class="p12-stat-green">+2,000W ★</span></div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- =====================================================
         CVT TRANSMISSION SECTION
         ===================================================== -->
    <hr class="p12-sep" />
    <div class="p12-section-label">Phase 12 · New Component — Static CVT Transmission</div>
    <h2 class="p12-section-title">The CVT Bridge — Engine runs at 2000 RPM, drivetrain doesn't know it</h2>

    <p class="p12-section-lead">
        A Continuously Variable Transmission (CVT) with a torque converter lock-up mounted between the static
        engine output shaft and the drivetrain. The engine holds medium idle RPM constantly — the CVT
        varies its ratio continuously to deliver only the output torque and speed the operator commands.
        At standstill with engine at medium idle: CVT ratio is near-infinite (output near zero). As
        speed increases, ratio steps down. The engine never drops below its self-sustaining RPM.
    </p>

    <div class="p12-cvt-grid">
        <div class="p12-cvt-card">
            <div class="p12-cvt-badge">CVT Input</div>
            <h3>From Static Engine</h3>
            <p>The static oil-bath engine's output shaft drives the CVT primary pulley/sheave at constant medium-idle RPM (~500 rotor RPM = ~2,000 generator-equiv). The engine does not care what the drivetrain demands — it maintains its RPM via the boiler throttle independently.</p>
            <div class="p12-cvt-stat"><span>Input RPM (constant)</span><span>~500–1,200 RPM</span></div>
            <div class="p12-cvt-stat"><span>Input torque</span><span>Variable (boiler PSI)</span></div>
            <div class="p12-cvt-stat"><span>Coupling type</span><span>Torque converter + lockup</span></div>
        </div>

        <div class="p12-cvt-card p12-cvt-center">
            <div class="p12-cvt-badge p12-badge-gold">CVT Unit</div>
            <h3>Static CVT Gearbox</h3>
            <p>A toroidal or belt-drive CVT with ratio range 0.1:1 to 6:1. PCW controller varies CVT ratio to maintain drivetrain output matching operator demand while engine holds medium idle. Oil-bathed — shares lubrication circuit with main engine block. No external power required for ratio actuation.</p>
            <div class="p12-cvt-stat"><span>Ratio range</span><span>0.1:1 → 6:1</span></div>
            <div class="p12-cvt-stat"><span>Control</span><span>PCW servo · 100Hz</span></div>
            <div class="p12-cvt-stat"><span>Efficiency</span><span>~91–94% at cruise</span></div>
            <div class="p12-cvt-stat"><span>Lubrication</span><span>Shared oil-bath circuit</span></div>
        </div>

        <div class="p12-cvt-card">
            <div class="p12-cvt-badge p12-badge-blue">CVT Output</div>
            <h3>To Drivetrain / Rails</h3>
            <p>Output shaft delivers whatever torque/speed the operator demands — from zero (standstill, CVT at max ratio, torque converter absorbing slip) to full cruise (CVT locked at optimal ratio). The 2 rails and 3 wheels per side see smooth power delivery regardless of what the engine is doing internally.</p>
            <div class="p12-cvt-stat"><span>Output at standstill</span><span>~0 RPM (TC slip)</span></div>
            <div class="p12-cvt-stat"><span>Output at cruise</span><span>Demand-matched</span></div>
            <div class="p12-cvt-stat"><span>Regen braking path</span><span>CVT → gen overspeed</span></div>
        </div>
    </div>

