    <hr class="p12-sep"/>
    <div class="p12-section-label">★ Phase 12 Highlight — Exhaust Eddy Current Recovery</div>
    <h2 class="p12-section-title">How the exhaust pipe becomes a continuous free generator</h2>

    <p class="p12-section-lead">
        After the two pistons fire, the exhaust steam exits at 120–180°C and high velocity.
        In Phase 11 this energy was simply lost to the condenser. Phase 12 routes this exhaust through
        a specialised pipe section wrapped in opposing permanent magnet coils. The conductive steam (or
        partially ionized at high temperature) moving through the alternating magnetic field induces
        eddy currents in the metallic pipe wall. Those currents are harvested via external pickup coils
        and rectified to DC. No turbines. No bearings. No maintenance. It produces power continuously
        on every piston stroke, at any RPM, even at idle — making it uniquely suited to solve the
        cold-valley problem.
    </p>

    <div class="p12-eddy-detail-grid">
        <div class="p12-eddy-card">
            <div class="p12-eddy-badge">Physics</div>
            <h3>Why This Works</h3>
            <p>A conducting fluid (or pipe wall containing eddy currents) moving through a static magnetic field experiences the Lorentz force on its charge carriers. In an exhaust pipe, the dominant conduction path is the steel/copper pipe wall itself — the moving hot steam creates a temperature gradient that sustains a small current in the wall, and the alternating B-field pattern from opposing coils creates a traveling-wave induction effect. Similar in principle to a linear induction motor, run in reverse.</p>
            <div class="p12-eddy-stat"><span>Primary conductor</span><span>Pipe wall (steel/Cu)</span></div>
            <div class="p12-eddy-stat"><span>Secondary effect</span><span>Ionized steam (high temp)</span></div>
            <div class="p12-eddy-stat"><span>Coil arrangement</span><span>Opposing pairs · 8–16 coils</span></div>
            <div class="p12-eddy-stat"><span>Rectification</span><span>Full-wave bridge → DC bus</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="p12-eddy-card">
            <div class="p12-eddy-badge p12-eddy-badge-b">Output Estimates</div>
            <h3>What to Expect</h3>
            <p>Output scales with exhaust temperature, pipe diameter, steam velocity, and magnet field strength. At idle (low piston speed, 90 PSI), expect the lower end of the range. At cruise, exhaust velocity rises and output climbs proportionally. The key advantage is that even at minimum idle — the hardest operating point — the eddy recovery still produces a steady trickle because the pistons are still firing at medium speed.</p>
            <div class="p12-eddy-stat"><span>Idle output estimate</span><span>~300W continuous</span></div>
            <div class="p12-eddy-stat"><span>Cruise output estimate</span><span>~500–600W continuous</span></div>
            <div class="p12-eddy-stat"><span>Full throttle estimate</span><span>~600–900W</span></div>
            <div class="p12-eddy-stat"><span>Back-pressure penalty</span><span>&lt;2% (minimal)</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="p12-eddy-card">
            <div class="p12-eddy-badge p12-eddy-badge-c">Implementation</div>
            <h3>How to Build It</h3>
            <p>The exhaust recovery sleeve is a bolt-on section of pipe ~300–400mm long inserted between the piston exhaust port and the condenser return. The coils are wound on a ceramic former (heat-resistant to 250°C) and the whole assembly is enclosed in a steel shielding jacket. The pickup coils feed into a small bridge rectifier and smoothing capacitor before connecting to the battery bus. Total added weight: ~4–6 kg. No active cooling required — the exhaust flow itself keeps temperatures within coil limits.</p>
            <div class="p12-eddy-stat"><span>Sleeve length</span><span>300–400mm</span></div>
            <div class="p12-eddy-stat"><span>Coil former</span><span>Ceramic · 250°C rated</span></div>
            <div class="p12-eddy-stat"><span>Added weight</span><span>~4–6 kg</span></div>
            <div class="p12-eddy-stat"><span>Phase 12 bench test</span><span>Priority ①</span></div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- =====================================================
         MEDIUM IDLE + CVT — DEEP DIVE
         ===================================================== -->
    <hr class="p12-sep"/>
    <div class="p12-section-label">Phase 12 · Core Innovation — Medium Idle + CVT</div>
    <h2 class="p12-section-title">Why medium idle solves the cold valley permanently</h2>

    <div class="p12-cvt-detail-grid">
        <div class="p12-cvt-detail-card">
            <div class="p12-cvt-detail-badge">The Old Problem</div>
            <h3>Why Low Idle Failed</h3>
            <p>A steam system designed to idle at low pressure must also idle its generator at low speed. At low speed, the generator cannot sustain the boiler. The boiler therefore needs external energy input continuously at idle — it can never be fully self-supporting. This created a permanent energy debt that no amount of recovery circuits could fully close.</p>
            <p>The only real fix is to ensure the generator always operates above its self-sustain threshold. That means the engine must never drop below that threshold either.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="p12-cvt-detail-card p12-cvt-detail-highlight">
            <div class="p12-cvt-detail-badge p12-badge-green">The Phase 12 Fix</div>
            <h3>Raise the Floor, Use a CVT</h3>
            <p>Set the minimum boiler pressure to 90 PSI. The PCW controller enforces this hard — the throttle physically cannot command lower pressure. At 90 PSI, the static engine internal rotors sit at ~500 RPM. The generator in low gear produces ~4,800W. The vacuum-jacketed boiler only needs 2,800W. The system is now always in surplus at idle.</p>
            <p>The CVT absorbs the "excess" mechanical energy from the engine running at medium idle when the drivetrain needs less. The engine doesn't know — or care — what the drivetrain demands. It runs at its ideal operating point continuously.</p>
            <div class="p12-cvt-stat-highlight"><span>Idle surplus</span><span>+2,000W (charges battery)</span></div>
            <div class="p12-cvt-stat-highlight"><span>CVT range</span><span>0.1:1 → 6:1</span></div>
            <div class="p12-cvt-stat-highlight"><span>Standstill handling</span><span>Torque converter slip</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="p12-cvt-detail-card">
            <div class="p12-cvt-detail-badge p12-badge-orange">CVT Trade-offs</div>
            <h3>What to Watch</h3>
            <p>A CVT adds mechanical complexity and a small efficiency loss (~6–9% at cruise vs direct drive). The oil-bath shared with the main engine reduces complexity but requires the shared oil to handle both engine lubrication viscosity and CVT belt/sheave requirements — may need a dual-grade synthetic oil or separate circuit with a shared cooler.</p>
            <p>At standstill with the torque converter absorbing full engine output, heat generation in the TC fluid is significant. A TC cooler loop from the radiator circuit handles this.</p>
            <div class="p12-cvt-stat-highlight"><span>CVT efficiency loss</span><span>6–9% at cruise</span></div>
            <div class="p12-cvt-stat-highlight"><span>TC heat at standstill</span><span>Cooler loop required</span></div>
            <div class="p12-cvt-stat-highlight"><span>Phase 12 bench test</span><span>Priority ②</span></div>
        </div>
    </div>

