            <strong>Phase 12 Concept Note — For Phase 13 Refinement</strong>
            <p>
                The cold-valley problem is now structurally solved rather than patched. By raising the minimum idle PSI to 90,
                the generator permanently operates above self-sustain threshold. The CVT absorbs the drivetrain
                mismatch without any awareness by the engine. The eddy current exhaust recovery is the highest-priority
                bench test — it requires no new power source, no moving parts, and produces power continuously proportional
                to exhaust velocity, meaning it always contributes most exactly when the system needs it most (high throttle).
                Phase 13 should focus on: (1) physical bench test of eddy coil sleeve on a representative exhaust pipe
                with measured steam flow, (2) CVT prototype or adaptation from commercial automotive unit, (3) vacuum-jacket
                boiler specification with ASME-rated inner vessel, (4) TEG zone layout on boiler exterior surface,
                (5) PCW firmware for PSI floor enforcement and CVT ratio servo loop.
                The MHD sleeve remains a Phase 13+ experimental concept — the K₂CO₃ seeding mechanism and
                ionization temperature requirements need laboratory validation before inclusion in a vehicle prototype.
                All power figures in Phase 12 remain theoretical — real losses from bearing friction, oil viscosity at
                temperature, steam condensation in the exhaust sleeve, and CVT belt slip will reduce actual figures.
                Conservative engineering margin of 20–30% should be applied to all output estimates before committing
                to hardware specifications.
            </p>
        </div>
